generation:
 The trial of baseball stars Barry Bonds and Roger Clemens, who face charges of lying to Congress about performance-enhancing drugs, has been delayed until April.

They are among seven athletes and sports figures who were scheduled to go on trial in Washington on 7 January.

US district judge Reggie Walton set the new date on Thursday, saying the delay was needed to give defence lawyers more time to prepare.

The other five defendants are:

Bill Bell, a strength coach;

Burton Abbott, a trainer;

Brian McNamee, a former trainer;

Bobby Estalella, a former Mets catcher;

Armando Rios, a former minor league pitcher.

They all pleaded not guilty to charges they lied to congressional investigators about steroid use in baseball.

Clemens and Bonds, baseball's all-time leader in career home runs, face charges they deceived Congress in 2003 about the use of performance-enhancing drugs.

Both athletes have denied taking steroids.
